@page "/SurvivingRecords"
@model SurvivingRecordsModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "The Surviving Record";
}

<div class="row justify-content-center g-4">
    <div class="col-xl-10">
        @await Html.PartialAsync("Components/_PageHeader", new baseball_history_web.ViewModels.PageHeaderModel
        {
            Title = "The Surviving Record",
            Subtitle = "Why some careers in this database are fragments — and why the fragments are worth showing.",
            Eyebrow = "History"
        })
    </div>

    <div class="col-xl-10">
        <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body p-4">
                <section>
                    <h2>Why some players are only a surname</h2>
                    <p>
                        This database holds 413 players with no recorded first name — eight of them known
                        only as Smith — and dozens more known by a bare initial. They appear throughout the
                        site with a <span class="partial-record-badge">Partial record</span> badge. They are
                        not data-entry errors. Each one played professional baseball, appeared in a box score
                        that survived, and left nothing else behind that researchers have yet found. Most
                        debuted between 1910 and 1949, in the segregated Black leagues; a smaller group dates
                        to the 1870s&ndash;1900s, when even the white professional game kept uneven books.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        A player's record surviving, in other words, was never neutral. It depended on who
                        wrote the games down, which newspapers printed them, and whose archives were kept.
                    </p>
                </section>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-xl-10">
        <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body p-4">
                <section>
                    <h2>A record kept by weekly newspapers</h2>
                    <p>
                        The National and American Leagues had daily press coverage in every city and, for
                        most of their history, central statistical bureaus tallying every game. Black
                        baseball had neither. Big-city dailies rarely printed Negro Leagues box scores;
                        the record fell mostly to the Black press &mdash; papers like the
                        <em>Chicago Defender</em> and <em>Pittsburgh Courier</em> &mdash; which published
                        weekly, not daily. A box score might run days after the game, without first names,
                        or not at all.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        League schedules were also only part of the season. Teams sustained themselves by
                        barnstorming &mdash; exhibition games, town-team challenges, winter play in Cuba and
                        Mexico &mdash; and none of that entered a league ledger. The official schedule ran
                        roughly 60&ndash;80 league games; the men on the field often played twice that many.
                    </p>
                </section>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-xl-10">
        <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body p-4">
                <section>
                    <h2>What the fragments mean: Josh Gibson</h2>
                    <p>
                        Josh Gibson's Hall of Fame plaque credits him with "almost 800 home runs" across
                        all competition &mdash; league games, exhibitions, and barnstorming tours together.
                        The documented league record in this database credits him with
                        <a href="/Players/gibsojo99">197 home runs and a .364 average across 1930&ndash;1946</a>.
                        Both numbers are honest; they measure different things. The plaque counts a career
                        as it was lived. The database counts the fraction of it that surviving box scores
                        can prove.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        That gap is the surviving-record problem in one player: the greatest documented
                        totals of the Negro Leagues systematically <em>understate</em> the careers behind
                        them, because the seasons were short and much of the play was never league-recorded.
                        Comparing a Negro Leagues counting stat against a 154-game AL/NL season head-to-head
                        misreads what the number represents.
                    </p>
                </section>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-xl-10">
        <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body p-4">
                <section>
                    <h2>The reconstruction, still underway</h2>
                    <p>
                        What survives today exists because researchers rebuilt it game by game. The
                        <a href="https://www.seamheads.com/NegroLgs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Seamheads
                        Negro Leagues Database</a>, led by Gary Ashwill and colleagues, reconstructs season
                        lines from digitized newspaper box scores &mdash; and totals still shift as more
                        papers are scanned and more games are found. In December 2020, Major League Baseball
                        recognized seven Negro Leagues (1920&ndash;1948) as major leagues, and in 2024 it
                        folded the reconstructed statistics into its official records, where Gibson's career
                        batting average now stands as the all-time mark.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        You can see the reconstruction's moving edge on this site: different editions of the
                        data disagree in small ways &mdash; an average a few points apart, a season found or
                        refined &mdash; not because anyone is wrong, but because the count is still being
                        taken, a century later.
                    </p>
                </section>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-xl-10">
        <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body p-4">
                <section>
                    <h2>How this site presents it</h2>
                    <p>
                        We show the documented record as it stands &mdash; raw totals,
                        <a href="/About#data-scope">not context-adjusted</a> &mdash; and mark its gaps
                        instead of smoothing them over. Players for whom only a partial name survived carry
                        the badge that brought you here. Treat every segregation-era total as a floor, not
                        a measure: the record that survived is the least these players did.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        Further reading: the
                        <a href="https://www.seamheads.com/NegroLgs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Seamheads Negro Leagues Database</a>,
                        the <a href="https://nlbm.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Negro Leagues Baseball Museum</a>,
                        and <a href="https://sabr.org/research/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SABR's research collections</a>.
                    </p>
                </section>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
